<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>Typed management client for AgentPrism, generated from the OpenAPI document published at https://agentprism.doayen.web.tr/openapi/agentprism.json. Covers every /api/* management operation. Takes no AgentPrism package and no NuGet package.</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(CommonPackageTags);client;openapi;http</PackageTags>
    <!--
      The public surface (160 generated operations, ~250 DTOs) is DERIVED
      from docs/openapi/agentprism.json, which already has its own drift gate
      (OpenApiSnapshotTests server-side, ClientCoverageTests here). Tracking
      it a second time through PublicApiAnalyzers would turn every
      `dotnet nswag run` regeneration into a multi-thousand-line
      PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt diff nobody reviews line by line - the same
      reasoning AgentPrism.Templates and AgentPrism.Generators already use
      for their own generated/non-authored surface. Decision: docs/KARARLAR.md.
    -->
    <AgentPrismPublicApiTrackingEnabled>false</AgentPrismPublicApiTrackingEnabled>
    <!--
      🚨 MEASURED (docs/arsiv/fazlar/83-TIPLI-ISTEMCI-VE-CLI.md, section 83.6/risk table):
      the AOT promise does NOT hold for the NSwag-generated client. Every
      request/response call is hand-wired to AgentPrismClientJsonContext (a
      source-generated JsonSerializerContext, see AgentPrismApiClient.JsonContext.cs)
      through JsonSerializerOptions.TypeInfoResolver - functionally reflection-free
      at runtime - but NSwag hardcodes the GENERIC
      JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(json, options)/SerializeToUtf8Bytes<T>(value, options)
      overloads at 42 call sites (39 request-body serializations plus the
      shared ReadObjectResponseAsync<T> helper, itself called from all 160
      operations) rather than the JsonTypeInfo<T>-based ones. The trim/AOT
      analyzer flags the generic overload unconditionally, regardless of what
      TypeInfoResolver is set at runtime, because it cannot prove type
      coverage statically - measured with -p:AgentPrismAotCompatible=true:
      146 IL2026/IL3050/IL2075 diagnostics across those 42 lines x 3 TFMs.
      Rewriting the generated call sites to typed JsonTypeInfo<T> overloads
      is out of proportion to what this package promises. K-006 is
      layer-based, not a global promise: AgentPrism.AspNetCore and
      AgentPrism.UI already opt out for their own reasons. Decision: docs/KARARLAR.md.
    -->
    <AgentPrismAotCompatible>false</AgentPrismAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    NO AgentPrism package is taken on (docs/arsiv/fazlar/83-TIPLI-ISTEMCI-VE-CLI.md, section
    83.6). The generated client's DTOs mirror the OpenAPI document, not
    AgentPrism.Abstractions - reusing Abstractions would fight the code
    generator and would leak server-side store interfaces (like IRunStore)
    into an HTTP consumer. Serialization uses raw System.Text.Json source
    generation (AgentPrismClientJsonContext.g.cs), which is part of the
    net8.0+ shared framework - no NuGet package for it.

    The ONE NuGet package taken is Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions,
    for AddAgentPrismClient(this IServiceCollection ...). It carries no
    transitive dependencies of its own and is already present, directly or
    transitively, in effectively every .NET app that uses DI.
    IHttpClientFactory (Microsoft.Extensions.Http) is deliberately NOT used -
    same reasoning as WebhookHttpClient (K-164): it would add a second
    package, and a consumer could reconfigure the named client and undo
    AddAgentPrismClient's setup. The HttpClient is constructed once and kept
    for the singleton's lifetime instead, the pattern Microsoft recommends
    when not going through the factory.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
